@anon.a2b023d1a4e2834952d16152dce23780.633 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [1432 x i8] }> <{ [1432 x i8] c"Draws augmentation lines in a matrix.\0A\0A- `{none}`: No lines are drawn.\0A- A single number: A vertical augmentation line is drawn\0A  after the specified column number. Negative numbers start from the end.\0A- A dictionary: With a dictionary, multiple augmentation lines can be\0A  drawn both horizontally and vertically. Additionally, the style of the\0A  lines can be set. The dictionary can contain the following keys:\0A  - `hline`: The offsets at which horizontal lines should be drawn.\0A    For example, an offset of `2` would result in a horizontal line\0A    being drawn after the second row of the matrix. Accepts either an\0A    integer for a single line, or an array of integers\0A    for multiple lines. Like for a single number, negative numbers start from the end.\0A  - `vline`: The offsets at which vertical lines should be drawn.\0A    For example, an offset of `2` would result in a vertical line being\0A    drawn after the second column of the matrix. Accepts either an\0A    integer for a single line, or an array of integers\0A    for multiple lines. Like for a single number, negative numbers start from the end.\0A  - `stroke`: How to [stroke]($stroke) the line. If set to `{auto}`,\0A    takes on a thickness of 0.05em and square line caps.\0A\0A```example\0A$ mat(1, 0, 1; 0, 1, 2; augment: #2) $\0A// Equivalent to:\0A$ mat(1, 0, 1; 0, 1, 2; augment: #(-1)) $\0A```\0A\0A```example\0A$ mat(0, 0, 0; 1, 1, 1; augment: #(hline: 1, stroke: 2pt + green)) $\0A```" }>, align 1
